WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.467
A subquery in SELECT must behave like a scalar for each output row.

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:06.167
If it is uncorrelated, the same value may repeat.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:09.492
If it is correlated, the value can change from row to row.

00:00:09.492 --> 00:00:15.502
This placement is useful when you want to enrich each row with one related summary value.

00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:18.257
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:18.257 --> 00:00:23.577
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:23.577 --> 00:00:25.757
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:25.757 --> 00:00:30.612
In a real setting such as a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists,

00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:36.202
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:37.552
before discussing syntax.

00:00:37.552 --> 00:00:42.800
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
